Z={
1:"Commentators note vijñāna-sahitam, “with realisation”: the teaching is not only to be understood but lived. Madhusūdana says freedom from evil, aśubha, comes by knowing it. Many devotees call chapter 9 the chapter of grace.",
2:"Commentators note dharmyam, “in keeping with dharma”: devotion does not oppose right living. Madhusūdana says the knowledge is avyaya, imperishable, because its fruit never fades. Rājavidyā has given its name to schools and institutions across India.",
3:"Commentators note parantapa, “scorcher of foes”: Kṛṣṇa reminds Arjuna of his strength even when warning. Madhusūdana says lack of faith keeps people circling in rebirth. Faith here is like a door: it must be opened from inside.",
4:"Commentators note avyakta-mūrtinā, “by my unmanifest form”: the Lord pervades all without being seen. Madhusūdana says beings exist in the Lord as pots exist in clay. A simple reflection: everything you see rests on something you cannot see.",
5:"Commentators note bhūta-bhāvana, “source of beings”: the Lord brings beings into existence. Madhusūdana says the mystery is called divine because reason alone cannot solve it. Some teachers say a mind that can hold a paradox calmly has begun to grow wise.",
6:"Commentators note mahān, “mighty”: the wind is strong, yet space is untouched. Madhusūdana says beings, however active, do not disturb the Lord. Many find this image helpful in times of turmoil: the vast stillness remains.",
7:"Commentators note māmikām prakṛtim, “my nature”: beings return to the Lord’s own nature at dissolution. Madhusūdana says they rest there like seeds in winter. The image is of a cosmic sleep and waking.",
8:"Commentators note avaṣṭabhya, “resting on” or “taking hold of”: the Lord governs nature. Madhusūdana says the Lord creates according to each being’s karma. The verse holds together divine sovereignty and human responsibility.",
9:"Commentators note dhanañjaya, “winner of wealth”: Kṛṣṇa calls Arjuna by a name earned in action as he speaks of unattached action. Madhusūdana says the Lord sits above his works like a neutral judge. The verse models how one can act fully without being bound.",
10:"Commentators note viparivartate, “revolves”: the world turns because of the Lord’s presence. Madhusūdana says nature acts, the Lord witnesses. Some compare the Lord to a magnet: it does not move, yet iron moves near it.",
11:"Commentators note bhūta-maheśvara, “great Lord of beings”: the unwise miss this in Kṛṣṇa. Many saints were dismissed in their lifetimes; the Ālvār Tiruppāṇ, from a community kept outside the temple, was carried into the Śrīraṅgam shrine, according to tradition, by the Lord’s own command.",
12:"Commentators note vicetasaḥ, “senseless”: the demonic lose their good sense. Madhusūdana says their hopes, actions and knowledge are all empty. The verse warns that a life cut off from its source becomes empty, however busy.",
13:"Commentators note bhūtādim avyayam, “imperishable origin of beings”: the great souls know the Lord as source. Madhusūdana says their minds rest nowhere else. Such people are found in every tradition: quiet, steady, kind.",
14:"Commentators note namasyantaḥ, “bowing”: devotion includes the body. Madhusūdana says singing, striving and bowing together make complete worship. The Bhāgavata later lists nine forms of devotion, and singing is the second of them.",
15:"Commentators note viśvato-mukham, “facing every direction”: the Lord is seen everywhere. Madhusūdana says those who worship through knowledge see the Lord as one with themselves, as distinct, or in all forms. The verse makes room for many philosophies.",
16:"Commentators note that kratu refers to the great Vedic rites like Jyotiṣṭoma, and yajña to the daily household offerings. Madhusūdana says the Lord is in both. Every element of worship, from the grandest to the simplest, belongs to him.",
17:"Commentators note pavitram, “purifier”: the Lord purifies all who approach. Madhusūdana says the Vedas are named because they are the Lord’s knowledge. The verse invites the devotee to see God as every relationship.",
18:"Commentators note that the twelve names in this verse are often used in daily prayer. Madhusūdana says each name answers a need: the lost need a goal, the weak a supporter, the afraid a refuge, the lonely a friend. The verse is a complete prayer in itself.",
19:"Commentators note amṛtam and mṛtyuḥ, immortality and death, both from the Lord. Madhusūdana says the Lord gives life to the righteous and death to the wicked according to karma. The verse invites acceptance of both joy and loss.",
20:"Commentators note pūta-pāpāḥ, “purified of sin”: the ritualists are good people. Madhusūdana says their reward is real heaven. The Gītā respects their path while pointing beyond it.",
21:"Commentators note gatāgatam, “coming and going”: the ritualists cycle between heaven and earth. Madhusūdana says this is the fate of those who desire desires. The verse warns against making even heaven the final goal.",
22:"Commentators note ananyāḥ, “with no other”: the devotee thinks of nothing else. Madhusūdana says the Lord personally cares for such a devotee. Many devotees find in this verse the courage to trust when the future is uncertain.",
23:"Commentators note śraddhayānvitāḥ, “endowed with faith”: sincere worship of any deity reaches the Lord. Madhusūdana says those who do not know this get limited results. The verse invites respect for all sincere worshippers.",
24:"Commentators note prabhuḥ, “lord”: the Lord is master of every sacrifice. Madhusūdana says knowing him as such turns ritual into devotion. The verse gives a simple practice: before any worship, remember who receives it.",
25:"Commentators note bhūtejyāḥ, “worshippers of spirits”: some worship ghosts and spirits for power. Madhusūdana says they reach what they worship. The Gītā does not forbid them, but shows the limits of their path.",
26:"Commentators note prayatātmanaḥ, “of the pure-hearted”: the offering must come from a pure heart. Madhusūdana says the Lord eats the offering of love. Śabarī, an old tribal woman in the Rāmāyaṇa, offered Rāma berries she had tasted to check their sweetness, and Rāma ate them with joy.",
27:"Commentators note yat karoṣi, “whatever you do”: no act is excluded. Madhusūdana says every act offered to the Lord becomes worship. The practice can begin with a single act each day.",
28:"Commentators note śubhāśubha-phalaiḥ, “from good and bad fruits”: offering frees from both. Madhusūdana says even good karma binds, and offering cuts that bond too. The verse links devotion with freedom.",
29:"Commentators note bhajanti tu, “but those who worship”: devotion creates intimacy. Madhusūdana says the Lord is like a wish-fulfilling tree that gives to those who approach. The tree is the same for all; those who come near receive.",
30:"Commentators note ananya-bhāk, “with undivided devotion”: the turning must be wholehearted. Madhusūdana says such a person is to be considered good because his resolve is right. The verse warns against judging people by their past.",
31:"Commentators note śaśvac-chāntiṃ nigacchati, “attains lasting peace”: the turned wrongdoer finds peace. Madhusūdana says the Lord asks Arjuna to declare it publicly. The verse is a promise and a call to share it.",
32:"Commentators note parāṃ gatim, “the supreme goal”: devotion opens the highest goal to all. Madhusūdana says birth does not limit devotion. The bhakti movements took this message across India: saints like Cokhāmeḷā, Nandanār and Ravidās came from communities long excluded from temples.",
33:"Commentators note rājarṣayaḥ, “royal sages”: Arjuna, a prince, is among them. Madhusūdana says the human birth is a rare chance for liberation. The verse urges using it well.",
34:"Commentators note yuktvā, “united”: the four acts together unite one with the Lord. Madhusūdana says this verse is the essence of bhakti. Many devotees recite it daily as a summary of the whole path.",
}
for _k,_t in Z.items():
    _a=DEEP.get(_k,('','',''))
    DEEP[_k]=(_a[0],(_a[1]+' '+_t).strip(),_a[2])
